Cross-sieve edge flips among local-prime obstruction tournaments
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
twin gap 2 vs Goldbach N=210:  3 flips
twin gap 2 vs Goldbach N=2110: 1 flips
Goldbach 210 vs Goldbach 2110: 4 flips
twin gap 2 vs Euler p=41:      62 flips
Interpretation: the same local primes reorder when the retained side channel
changes.  That is the tournament signal, not noise.

Hard-problem morale ledger
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
problem        vertices to tournamentize          concrete finite morale move
Twin primes    local primes/residue lanes          rank bottleneck primes; compare gaps
Goldbach       local primes vs target N            track one-lane vs two-lane primes
RH             prime-power/zero phase terms        orient explicit-formula contributors
BSD            bad primes/root-number factors      orient local-global rank obligations
Collatz        residue classes/valuation jumps     orient drift witnesses by descent
Heegner        boundary/interior proof slots       use S650 endpoint horizon witnesses
LRC/UD         shell or spine/bulk side channels   keep quotient-loss labels attached

Tournament Analysis
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
challenged vertices = integers, primes, residues, valuations, characters,
  Euler factors, zero terms, class groups, proof obligations, and side channels
chosen vertices = proof-carrier methods
pairwise observable = which method preserves more local witness information
switch/gauge = orient A -> B when A gives smaller finite probes with less scalar collapse
